{"type":"message","message":{"id":"msg_3311fabc-ae62-4901-b04d-8cc109a536f6","role":"user","created":1775333378,"content":[{"type":"toolResponse","id":"call_d961fe5af0404fd89a7462ba","toolResult":{"status":"success","value":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"136:1/ Gross receipts less appropriations to the Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund and refunds of receipts.\n151:| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Internal revenue 1/ > Income and profits taxes > Withheld by employers 2/ | Internal revenue 1/ > Income and profits taxes > Other 3/ | Internal revenue 1/ > Income and profits taxes > Total 4/ | Internal revenue 1/ > Employment taxes 2/ > Unnamed: 4_level_2 | Customs > Customs > Unnamed: 5_level_2 | Other receipts 6/ > Other receipts 6/ > Unnamed: 6_level_2 | Gross receipts > Gross receipts > Unnamed: 7_level_2 | Deductions > Miscellaneous internal revenue > Unnamed: 8_level_2 | Deductions > Total internal revenue > Unnamed: 9_level_2 | Net receipts > Net receipts > Unnamed: 10_level_2 | Unnamed: 11_level_0 > Appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund 7/ > Unnamed: 11_level_2 | Unnamed: 12_level_0 > Refunds of receipts 8/ > Unnamed: 12_level_2 |\n211:Source: Actual figures from Daily Treasury Statement; estimates based on 1954 Budget document, released January 9, 1953. Expenditure classifications shown here differ somewhat from those in the Budget. 1/ 2/ For further detail, see tables under \"Internal Revenue Collections\". Under Current Tax Payment Act of 1943, as amended (26 U.S.C. 1621-1632). Monthly figures include old-age insurance taxes on employers and employees, because these taxes are not separable currently from income tax withheld. Fiscal year figures exclude old-age insurance taxes, on the basis of estimates beginning 1951. For further explanation, see footnote 7. 3/ Corporation income and profits taxes and individual income tax not withheld. Monthly figures include old-age insurance tax on self-employment income, because this tax is not separable currently from income tax not withheld. Fiscal year figures exclude old-age\n215:Consists of receipts for old-age insurance, unemployment insurance, and railroad retirement; for further detail, see Tables 7 and 8. \n216:Beginning January 1951, receipts for old-age insurance are estimated as explained in footnote 7. Railroad unemployment insurance contributions for administrative expenses are included in \"Other receipts\". \n289:1/ For basis of appropriations, see Table 7. Amounts appropriated are equivalent to the amounts of taxes collected and deposited for old-age insurance. The Social Security Act Amendments of 1950 (Public Law 734), approved August 28, 1950, changed in certain respects the basis of transferring the appropriated funds to the trust fund. Effective January 1, 1951, the old-age insurance taxes on employers and employees and the withheld income tax are paid into the Treasury in combined amounts without separation as to type of tax. The old-age insurance tax on self-employment income, imposed by Public Law 734, is levied and collected as part of the individual income tax, beginning with the taxable year 1951. Beginning January 1951, the amounts transferred currently as appropriations to the trust fund are based on estimates of old-age insurance tax receipts made by the Secretary of the Treasury, and are adjusted in later transfers on the basis of wage and self-employment income records maintained by the Federal\n291:Security Administrator. For purposes of this table, the amounts credited to the trust fund beginning January 1951 are considered to represent estimated receipts of old-age insurance taxes for corresponding periods. Accordingly, these amounts are included under employment taxes and excluded from total income and profits taxes as shown. Throughout the calendar year 1951, the same amounts were deducted from the combined taxes paid in by employers, and the balance was shown as income tax withheld. Beginning January 1952, the tax on self-employment income is allowed for in the amounts credited to the trust fund, but no breakdown between the two types of old-age insurance taxes is available monthly. Estimates of the breakdown are made on a fiscal year basis beginning 1952. Interest on refunds is included in Table 5 under \"Miscellaneous\". Since monthly figures in these two columns include old-age insurance taxes (see footnotes 2 and 3), they will not add to the total, which excludes those taxes.\n455:2/ Gross receipts less appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund and refunds of receipts.\n651:Appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund\n694:Under Current Tax Payment Act of 1943, effective July 1, 1943, as amended (26 U.S.C. 1621-1632). Victory taxes withheld pursuant to the Revenue Act of 1942 are included in \"Other income and profits taxes\". Monthly and calendar year figures include old-age insurance taxes on employers and employees beginning January 1952, because those taxes are not thereafter separable currently from income tax withheld.\n695:Fiscal year figures exclude old-age insurance taxes, on the basis of estimates beginning 1951. For further explanation, see footnote 8.\n697:For adjustments reflected in these figures, see footnote 9. Corporation income and profits taxes and individual income tax not withheld. Monthly and calendar year figures include old-age insurance tax on self-employment income beginning with returns filed for taxable year 1951, because this tax is not separable currently from individual income tax. Fiscal year figures exclude old-age insurance tax, on the basis of estimates beginning 1952. For further explanation, see footnote 8.\n701:Consists of receipts for old-age insurance under the Federal Insurance Contributions Act, unemployment insurance under the Federal Unemployment Tax Act (taxes on employers of 8 or more), and railroad retirement under the Railroad Retirement Act (taxes on carriers and their employees). Railroad unemployment insurance contributions for administrative expenses are included in \"Other receipts\". Old-age insurance taxes are partly estimated, beginn- ing January 1951 (see footnote 8).\n705:Appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund are equivalent to amounts of taxes collected and deposited for old-age insurance (42 U.S.C. 401 (a)). The Social Security Act Amendments of 1950 (Public Law 734), approved August 28, 1950, changed in certain respects the basis of transferring the appropriated funds to the trust fund. Effective January 1, 1951, the old-age insurance taxes on employers and employees and the withheld income tax are paid into the Treasury in combined amounts without separation as to type of tax. The old-age insurance tax on self-employment income, imposed by Public Law 734, is levied and collected as part of the individual income tax, beginning with the taxable year 1951. Beginning January 1951, the amounts transferred currently as appropriations to the trust fund are based on estimates of old-age insurance tax receipts made by the Secretary of the Treasury, and are adjusted in later transfers on the basis of wage and self-employment income records maintained by the Federal Security Administrator. For purposes of this table, the amounts credited to the trust fund beginning January 1951 are considered to represent estimated receipts of old-age insurance taxes for corresponding periods. Accordingly, these amounts are included under employment taxes and excluded from total income and profits taxes as shown. Throughout the calendar year 1951, the same amounts were deducted from the combined taxes paid in by employers, and the balance was shown as income tax withheld. Beginning January 1952, the tax on self-employment income is allowed for in the amounts credited to the trust fund, but no breakdown between the two types of old-age insurance taxes is available monthly. Estimates of the breakdown are made on a fiscal year basis beginning 1952\n1269:| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Excess of net receipts, or expenditures (-) 1/ > Excess of net receipts, or expenditures (-) 1/ > Unnamed: 1_level_2 | Receipts > Total > Unnamed: 2_level_2 | Receipts > Gross receipts > Social security taxes For old-age insurance 2/ | Receipts > Gross receipts > For unemployment insurance 3/ | Deductions > Appropriations to Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund 4/ > Unnamed: 5_level_2 | Deductions > Refunds of social security taxes 5/ > Unnamed: 6_level_2 | Net receipts > Net receipts > Unnamed: 7_level_2 |\n1292:| Fiscal year or month > Fiscal year or month > Unnamed: 0_level_2 | Total > Total > Unnamed: 1_level_2 | Administrative expenses > Administrative expenses > Unnamed: 2_level_2 | Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund > Transfers to F.O.A. and S.I. Trust Fund > Unnamed: 3_level_2 | Grants to States > Total > Unnamed: 4_level_2 | Grants to States > Social Security Administration > Old-age assistance | Grants to States > Social Security Administration > Aid to dependent children | Grants to States > Social Security Administration > Aid to the blind | Grants to States > Children's Bureau > Aid to disabled 8 | Grants to States > Children's Bureau > Maternal and child health | Grants to States > Children's Bureau > Crippled children | Grants to States > Bureau of Employment Security > Child welfare | Unnamed: 12_level_0 > Unnamed: 12_level_1 > 9/ |\n1319:4/ Appropriation to the Federal Old-Age and Survivors Insurance Trust Fund equivalent to the amount of taxes collected and deposited for\n1321:old-age insurance (42 U.S.C. 401 (a); see also footnote 2). The Social Security Act Amendments of 1950 (Public Law 734), approved August 28, 1950, changed in certain respects the basis of transferring the appropriated funds to the trust fund. Effective January 1, 1951, the amounts transferred currentl